# Holes And Infoholes

An infohole should not enter the manual as a black-hole metaphor. The lawful
ascent is older and stricter: support, restriction, failure to glue, defect,
boundary, persistence, and only then an information-bearing hole.

The claim-site carried here is:

* claim-site: holes as boundary-supported non-effective descent, with infohole
  as a named frontier promotion target;
* phase: fibered and cohomological matter, after covers, restrictions,
  sections, and obstruction are lawful;
* generating sites: pairwise-Stone clopens, support masks, restriction,
  sheaf separation, defect as nonzero classifying invariant, and non-effective
  descent;
* downstream uses: comparison-atlas entries, frontier lab diagnostics, the Book VIII
  finite no-readout/shroud carrier, material boundary layers, and future
  info-object pages;
* Lean carriers: `Infomaterial.Site.Basic`,
  `Infomaterial.Site.Section`, `Infomaterial.Site.Sheaf`, and
  `Infomaterial.Cohomology.Defect`;
* Blueprint/proof-tree nodes: no Blueprint node yet asserts a full
  descent-and-effects `Infohole`. The page records the Lean prerequisites that
  such a node would need to cite;
* provenance: the manual Page Law, the descent discipline, and frontier
  discussion of information-bearing holes as mereological holes rather than
  imported spacetime objects;
* familiar comparison: mereological holes, boundary objects, defects, Cech
  obstruction, punctures, vacancies, and black holes only as a later causal
  projection;
* reader closure: distinguish a gap, a defect, a hole, and an infohole before
  reading any physical analogy;
* frontier status: a finite no-readout/shroud subobject is generated later in
  `Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Boundary.Infohole`, but the full
  descent-and-effects `Infohole` remains frontier. Promotion requires a record
  tying support boundary, non-fillability, persistence, and effects into one
  generated object.

The governing sentence is:

> An infohole is a persistent unfilled descent site whose boundary and effects
> are generated by the surrounding infostructure.

This page therefore refuses two shortcuts. A missing page is not an infohole;
that is just missing documentation. A black-hole reading is not an infohole
either; it is one possible downstream reading after causal boundary and
no-readout structure are generated.

## Gap, Defect, Hole, Infohole

The ladder is:

* a gap is absent data with no obstruction witness;
* a defect is a witnessed obstruction;
* a hole is an obstruction with a boundary or support that determines how the
  absence is encountered;
* an infohole is a persistent hole whose boundary, failure to fill, and
  force-like downstream effects are generated inside an infostructure.

Only the middle pieces are settled at this phase. Book VIII later generates a
causal no-readout/shroud subobject. The full descent-and-effects `Infohole`
carrier remains frontier.

## Support Before Boundary

The first lawful boundary-like material is support. A clopen inclusion lets one
read a smaller support inside a larger one:

{docstring Infomaterial.Site.meet_of_sub}

Checked:

```lean
example {U V : Clopen} (h : V ⊑ U) :
    meet U V = V :=
  meet_of_sub h
```

Restriction makes the support operative:

{docstring Infomaterial.AssocSheaf.restrict_vanishes}

{docstring Infomaterial.AssocSheaf.restrict_id}

Checked:

```lean
example (m : PredicationCell -> Bool) (c : Cochain) :
    Vanishes m (restrict m c) :=
  restrict_vanishes m c

example (c : Cochain) :
    restrict (fun _ => true) c = c :=
  restrict_id c
```

This is still not a hole. It is the ability to say where a section is being
read and where it vanishes.

## Separation Before Interior

A sheaf cover can determine a global section from local agreement:

{docstring Infomaterial.Sheaf.separated}

Checked:

```lean
example (c : Sheaf.Cover) (v w : Valuation)
    (h : forall p,
      p ∈ c.patches -> Sheaf.agreeOn p v w) :
    v = w :=
  Sheaf.separated c v w h
```

This is the positive side of descent. If all local pieces agree on a cover, the
global section is determined. A hole is not this theorem. A hole appears when the
local boundary data is real but no filling object has been promoted.

## Defect As Obstruction

The library already names one obstruction carrier:

{docstring Infomaterial.Cohomology.Defect.HasDefect}

Defects exist:

{docstring Infomaterial.Cohomology.Defect.defect_exists}

and a defect forces curvature and non-vanishing cohomology:

{docstring Infomaterial.Cohomology.Defect.hasDefect_curved}

{docstring Infomaterial.Cohomology.Defect.hasDefect_nonvanishing}

Checked:

```lean
example :
    exists v : Persistence.Aperiodic.Settling,
      v.regime = ClosureRegime.aperiodic
        ∧ Defect.HasDefect v :=
  Defect.defect_exists

example (v : Persistence.Aperiodic.Settling)
    (h : Defect.HasDefect v) :
    Flow.CurvatureRegime.IsCurved v.regime :=
  Defect.hasDefect_curved v h

example (v : Persistence.Aperiodic.Settling)
    (h : Defect.HasDefect v) :
    cohomologyVanishes v.regime = false :=
  Defect.hasDefect_nonvanishing v h
```

This is why "hole" cannot be a bare absence. The obstruction must be witnessed.
The current defect carrier witnesses nonzero classifying invariant. It does not
yet package boundary, interior, persistence, and effects as one object.

## What An Infohole Would Need

A promoted descent-and-effects `Infohole` carrier would have to package at
least:

* an ambient infostructure or site;
* a support boundary, probably as a clopen or masked family;
* local boundary sections or effects;
* a proof that the proposed interior is not effectively filled by the current
  descent data;
* a persistence condition saying the hole survives the relevant closure or
  flow;
* an effects map explaining the force-like behavior induced on surrounding
  sections;
* a theorem separating it from ordinary missing data.

That is the mathematical difference between "we have not documented something"
and "there is a hole." Missing data has no boundary theorem. A defect has an
obstruction witness. An infohole needs both obstruction and generated boundary,
plus persistence and effects.

## Later Projections

After this page, several later phrases become better disciplined:

* a causal no-readout region becomes a generated spacetime subobject in Book
  VIII only after causal boundary is lawful;
* a keratinous boundary layer may be a material response to an infohole only
  after contact locks and boundary support are welded;
* a black-hole analogy is admissible only as a projection of no-readout and
  non-effective descent, never as the source definition;
* an info-object is not automatically an infohole. It must be identified by the
  missing filling as much as by the boundary that surrounds it.

The current manual can therefore use `infohole` in two registers: the Book VIII
finite no-readout/shroud carrier is settled as a causal subobject, while the
unified descent, persistence, and effects carrier remains frontier. Resemblance
between those registers is not a theorem until the missing bridge is built.
